          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry to hear about your mother. Heart failure is a serious condition, but it is not the same as the heart stopping.\n\nAccording to the General clinical reference's heart failure summary (condition-heart-failure), heart failure means the heart cannot fill with or pump out blood well enough to meet the body's needs. It is a long-term condition that is usually managed over many years. Doctors often describe it using the ejection fraction, which is the share of blood the heart's main pumping chamber pushes out with each beat. Preserved ejection fraction (50 percent or above) is about half of cases. It is a different problem from weak pumping, not a milder form.\n\nThe search results did not include information on symptoms, treatments, or how family members can help. I can't answer those from the materials I have. Your mother's own clinician is the best source for her situation, including her ejection fraction type, her treatment plan, and what changes should prompt a call to them. If you have questions, you could write them down and bring them to her next appointment.\n\nIf your mother has sudden severe breathlessness, chest pain, fainting, or confusion, get emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
