          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does she have to weigh herself every day?",
          "answer": "Daily weighing is a way to spot fluid build-up early. According to the Diet and fluid in heart failure summary (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet), weight is a proxy for fluid, and it changes faster than swelling or breathlessness does. The same scale, at the same time of day (after waking and using the bathroom, before eating), is recommended so the numbers can be compared from day to day. That summary says a gain of a few pounds over two or three days, or a larger gain over a week, is the usual signal to contact a clinician before symptoms appear.\n\nThe Heart failure summary (condition-heart-failure) lists daily weights as part of self-management, along with recognizing early fluid retention and knowing when to seek help. It also says rapid weight gain over a few days needs urgent assessment.\n\nThe search did not cover what specific weight gain amount her own care team wants her to act on, or what to do with the numbers. Her clinician or nurse can give her those exact thresholds and tell her who to call.\n\nIf your mother has sudden severe breathlessness, breathlessness at rest or when lying down, chest pain, fainting, or confusion, get emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
