          "turn_index": 2,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about salt?",
          "answer": "The salt guidance I found is general. It doesn't give a specific daily amount.\n\nAccording to the Diet and fluid in heart failure summary (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet), sodium holds water in the body. In heart failure, a heart that can't move that extra fluid forward lets it back up into the lungs and legs. That is why a single high-salt meal can cause breathlessness within a day or two. The summary says the usual sodium targets are in a similar range to those for blood pressure. It also says most sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food rather than from the salt shaker.\n\nI also need to correct my previous answer. I said the Diet and fluid summary gives a weight gain of \"a few pounds over two or three days\" as the usual signal to call a clinician. That figure is not in the passages I retrieved for this question, so I can't confirm it from the sources. Please don't rely on it. Her care team should give her the exact weight thresholds.\n\nThe search did not return her specific sodium limit, which depends on her condition and any other health issues. Her doctor, nurse, or a dietitian can give her a personal target and tell her how to read food labels.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
